WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:00.720
Welcome!

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.560
We are here to discuss the best kind of paper!

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.040
You know, those papers that are published&nbsp;
on arxiv just before the end of the year.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.040
Because that is when everybody&nbsp;
wants to finish stuff but does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.040
not directly have a conference to submit to!
So great! Christmas reading papers!... Or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.040
Well, it does not matter how much you&nbsp;
like Christmas and new years eve papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.840
since this one here comes with breaking news!&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.840
The Transformers are here to&nbsp;
take over computer vision!

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.160
Ah, no, this is actually old news.
[mip]

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:48.560
We had a previous video about the ViT Vision&nbsp;
Transformer by Google that achieved sota on image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:56.160
recognition with a pure transformer architecture&nbsp;
-- but by also using 300 million more images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:03.040
of their own, not publicly released data. Find&nbsp;
the link to the video in the description below.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:11.840
Then the actual news of this video is, that&nbsp;
Facebook now managed to train a pure Transformer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:19.120
too, but unlike Google, they trained only on&nbsp;
ImageNet and achieved competitive results.&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.520
In this video we will discuss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.040
Why is this surprising, or even an&nbsp;
achievement from Facebook research?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.480
How did they do it? And… What does it mean&nbsp;
for transformer training on academic datasets?

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.800
As mentioned in the previous video about Google’s&nbsp;
Vision Transformer, the Transformer architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:49.520
is more general than CNNs or LSTMs, for example.&nbsp;
CNNs already know how to look at images:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.080
patch by patch with convolutions containing&nbsp;
the same parameters for every patch.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.720
LSTMs on the other hand, know how to&nbsp;
handle sequences: from left to right.&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.360
This inductive bias is intrinsically&nbsp;
bound to their architecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.520
But the Transformer does not&nbsp;
have these inductive biases:

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.720
for it, the data does not have to be a 2d&nbsp;
matrix (an image) or a sequence like text,&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.400
as long as it is a set.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.200
The order, if there is one, is&nbsp;
encoded in the positional embeddings.&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:30.320
Hence the transformer can do computer vision and&nbsp;
text processing or both combined by learning how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.320
to handle the visual or textual domains
from the data itself.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.520
But for learning this, it has to see more&nbsp;
data than the CNN or LSTM counterparts.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.640
Then of course it is not surprising&nbsp;
that Google’s ViT had to look at 300&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:53.280
million more images to beat state-of-the art&nbsp;
(88.55) on Imagenet on image recognition.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:03.520
The Facebook DeiT’s number of 84.4% on ImageNet&nbsp;
means a lot -- With around 300 times less data --

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.040
Crazy, how did they do it?

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.440
Facebook’s DeiT model processes images&nbsp;
very similarly to Google’s ViT model:&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.080
16 by 16 image patches are&nbsp;
transformed to "image vectors";&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.280
because vectors are the representations&nbsp;
that transformer naturally work with.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.520
The authors of the DeiT paper call these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.240
image vectors "patch tokens".&nbsp;
Call them whatever you like.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.360
These patch tokens are processed by Transformer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.640
layers, built by the usual multi-head&nbsp;
self-attention and feed forward layers&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.000
that in the end have to predict&nbsp;
the correct label of the image,&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.120
prediction which is enforced&nbsp;
by the cross entropy loss.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:56.480
But here one can see something that Google’s&nbsp;
ViT model did not have: A distillation token.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:03.520
This special token has to be predicted by&nbsp;
a transformer, to match the output of a CNN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.080
teacher model.
Why a CNN?

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.160
Because it has more priors about images.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.480
Therefore, the teacher CNN model can learn from&nbsp;
less data more easily than a transformers which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.720
without the inductive bias has more degrees&nbsp;
of freedom and needs more data to train.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.040
Distillation is a cool idea to apply for&nbsp;
the transformer here, which, simultaneously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.720
but with two different heads, has to both maximize&nbsp;
the crossentropy loss to make correct predictions,&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.880
and also make the teacher’s prediction.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.720
This is distillation in&nbsp;
the way of the Transformer.&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:39.680
This is the way.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:48.400
But unlike ViT of the Google Researchers, DeiT&nbsp;
from Facebook does not work with a lot of data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.480
And to alleviate this, the authors apply three&nbsp;
common techniques for CNNs on Transformers:&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.480
data augmentation, optimzsation&nbsp;
and regularization.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.120
For data augmentation, the authors do not&nbsp;
provide that many details, except that almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:10.320
every data-augmentation technique is useful.
If you are not familiar with data augmentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:18.160
for images, here are some examples: flipping&nbsp;
the image, on both axes when it makes sense,&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.680
or slightly changing the contrast or&nbsp;
brightness or saturation are useful ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:29.920
to change the image in the “eyes” of the neural&nbsp;
network, but without changing it’s meaning.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:37.200
Now, about the optimizers: Because transformers&nbsp;
are very sensitive to different optimizers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.160
the authors choose the best one and&nbsp;
search for the best hyperparameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.000
like the learning rates and&nbsp;
weight decay by cross-validation.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.080
Well, I mean for this, one needs the&nbsp;
Facebook computing power, right … ?

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.400
Furthermore, after training, they fine-tune the&nbsp;
transformer on images of a greater resolution than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:10.080
during training. They train on 224 by 224 pixel&nbsp;
images and fine-tune on 384 by 384 pixel images.&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:17.040
By this, even though the number of instances in&nbsp;
the training data is the same, for the network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.520
the effective training data is increased,&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:25.200
because the scaled higher-resolution&nbsp;
images are new for the network.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.520
Do not forget, that these images are then&nbsp;
converted to "patch tokens" or "image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:37.440
vectors" as Ms. Coffee Bean likes to call them,&nbsp;
so the Transformer can work with these vectors.&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.800
But in order to make this work properly,&nbsp;
the authors need to upscale the images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.840
with a special bicubic interpolation&nbsp;
with a specific regularisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.240
that preserves the norm of the image vectors.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:59.280
If they would not do so, the norm of the original&nbsp;
image vectors would be too different to the norm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:05.040
of the to the upscaled image and the trained&nbsp;
transformer would not recognize the relatedness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.360
between the data seen during training&nbsp;
and what it gets during fine-tuning.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:17.840
Ok, so to generalize and conclude:
DeiT is like ViT but, I cite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.960
“trained with a procedure more&nbsp;
adapted to a data-starving regime.”

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:30.000
And this is the cool thing about it, leaving ViTs&nbsp;
state of the art numbers on ImageNet a little in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.760
the shadow. Especially now that it seems like&nbsp;
Transformers are here to stay, it is great that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.840
a step towards data-efficiency was made by DeiT,
making it possible to train a transformer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:48.800
on image datasets of millions of images&nbsp;
instead of hundreds of millions of images.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:49.840
And this is great!

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.360
Perhaps you remember what Miss Coffee Bean said in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.840
the ViT video about Transformers&nbsp;
taking over CNNs, perhaps not.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:04.640
But I can tell you that she is very surprised&nbsp;
by this DeiT paper coming so quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.720
after ViT and addressing the data-efficiency&nbsp;
aspect of visual transformers right away.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.080
She really thought this might take longer.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.800
But… it’s machine learning, what did you expect?

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.560
You turn your head for a second and&nbsp;
a next an awesome thing happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:26.080
how can one keep up with this?

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.040
But rather how to not be overly excited by this?

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:31.440
Aaa?

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:32.800
Ms. Coffee Bean, are you done?

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:35.200
Yes, I am.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.840
Ok bye!

